# `distr::StudentT`

The Student t distribution: a bell-shaped curve with heavier tails than the normal, controlled by its degrees of freedom.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series distr::pdf(dst::StudentT, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logpdf(dst::StudentT, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::cdf(dst::StudentT, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logcdf(dst::StudentT, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::sample(dst::StudentT, const std::vector<double>& params, std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes, pluss::Config cfg = {}, std::uint64_t seed = 0);
```

## What It Does

The Student t distribution arises when a normal sample mean is standardised by an estimated standard deviation, and is the standard heavy-tailed model for small samples and for returns. Its single shape parameter, the degrees of freedom `nu`, controls the tails: `nu = 1` is the Cauchy distribution, the variance exists only for `nu > 2` and equals `nu/(nu - 2)`, and as `nu` grows the distribution approaches the normal.

The kind-based functions take `{nu}` (location 0, scale 1) or `{nu, location, scale}`; the object `distr::StudentT{degrees_of_freedom, location, scale}` takes all three.

The parameters, in the order of the `params` list:

| Parameter | Constraint | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `nu` | > 0 | the degrees of freedom; small values give heavy tails, large values approach the normal |
| `location` | any real | the centre |
| `scale` | > 0 | the scale; the standard deviation is `scale sqrt(nu/(nu - 2))` for `nu > 2` |

Support: all real numbers.

The object form of this family, with the same functions as methods, is on its own page: [`distr::StudentT`](../object_based/StudentT.md).

## Parameters

`const pluss::Series& x`
: The points at which to evaluate, a `Series` of any shape; the result has the same shape. Points outside the support have density zero and distribution value 0 or 1.

`const std::vector<double>& params`
: The parameters of the family, in the order shown in the table above (a shorter list selects the defaults: `{nu}` gives location 0 and scale 1). A list of the wrong length is refused.

`std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes`
: The shape of the array of random draws, for example `{n}` or `{rows, columns}`.

`pluss::Config cfg`
: The dtype and platform of the draws; the default is Float32 on the CPU, so request Float64 when precision matters.

`std::uint64_t seed`
: The seed of the stream. The same seed always gives the same draws; a seed of 0 is a fixed default, not a random one.

## Returns

`pdf`, `logpdf`, `cdf` and `logcdf` return a `Series` of the shape of `x`; `sample` returns a `Series` of the requested shape.

## Errors

- Parameters outside their valid range are refused with an error that names the requirement (`nu` and `scale` must be positive).
- A `params` list of the wrong length is refused.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Example 1 evaluates the density (mass) function, the distribution function and the log-density of the StudentT on a small grid with `distr::pdf`, `distr::cdf` and `distr::logpdf`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);

  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-6.0, -2.0, 0.0, 1.0, 3.0, 8.0}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> params = {5.0, 0.0, 1.0};

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "pdf:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::StudentT, x, params).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf:";
  for (double v : distr::cdf(dst::StudentT, x, params).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "logpdf:";
  for (double v : distr::logpdf(dst::StudentT, x, params).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

The density is largest on this grid at x = 0, where it is 0.37961; the distribution function runs from 9.20e-04 at x = -6 to 0.99975 at x = 8, and it is the running total of the density. `logpdf` is the logarithm of the density, for example log(0.37961) = -0.96861 at x = 0.

### Example 2

Example 2 checks that the three descriptions of the same probability agree: the integral of the density (sum of the masses) over a range, the difference of the distribution function at its ends, and the fraction of random draws that land in it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);
  const std::vector<double> params = {5.0, 0.0, 1.0};

  // The three ways to the probability that the variable lies in [-3, 3]:
  const auto grid = pluss::series::linspace(-3.0, 3.0, 3001, f64);
  const double area = as_f64(distr::pdf(dst::StudentT, grid, params).antidiff(grid).trapezoid());

  const auto edges = pluss::make_series({-3.0, 3.0}, f64);
  const auto c = distr::cdf(dst::StudentT, edges, params).items<double>();

  const auto draws = distr::sample(dst::StudentT, params, {200000}, f64, 7ULL);
  const double fraction = as_f64(((draws >= -3.0).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64) * (draws <= 3.0).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64)).mean());

  std::cout << "integral of the density: " << area << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf(3) - cdf(-3): " << c[1] - c[0] << '\n';
  std::cout << "fraction of 200000 draws in range: " << fraction << '\n';
}
```

The integral of the density (0.9699) and the difference of the distribution function (0.9699) agree to all printed digits: they are two computations of one probability. The fraction of random draws (0.97032) differs from it by 4.20e-04, which is the size of the sampling error of a fraction of 200000 draws (a standard error of about 3.82e-04), so the density, the distribution function and the sampler describe the same distribution.

### Example 3

Example 3 shows how the shape changes with the parameters: the density at the same points for 3 parameter settings.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-4.0, -2.0, 0.0, 2.0, 4.0}, f64);

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "nu 1   :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::StudentT, x, {1.0}).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "nu 4   :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::StudentT, x, {4.0}).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "nu 30  :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::StudentT, x, {30.0}).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

Read down the rows: `nu 1` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 0.3183); `nu 4` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 0.375); `nu 30` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 0.3956). The parameters move the location, the width and the shape of the curve, which is what choosing a member of the family means.

### Example 4

Example 4 confirms the convergence to the normal: with `nu = 100000` the Student t density and distribution function differ from the standard normal's by about a millionth.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::scientific << std::setprecision(2);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-3.0, -1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 3.0}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> here = {100000.0};
  const std::vector<double> there = {0.0, 1.0};

  const double dpdf = as_f64((distr::pdf(dst::StudentT, x, here) - distr::pdf(dst::Normal, x, there)).abs().max());
  const double dcdf = as_f64((distr::cdf(dst::StudentT, x, here) - distr::cdf(dst::Normal, x, there)).abs().max());
  std::cout << "largest difference in the density: " << dpdf << '\n';
  std::cout << "largest difference in the distribution function: " << dcdf << '\n';
}
```

The two largest differences are 1.21e-06 for the density and 1.21e-06 for the distribution function over the grid: small and shrinking as the approximation improves, so the two descriptions agree to the accuracy shown.

